{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 proof that both of Tokareva's conjectures on bent functions are false, following Feng, Jiang, Li, Li and Tang (arXiv:2610.32239). The bent-sum conjecture says that, for even n, every Boolean function on 𝔽₂ⁿ of algebraic degree at most n/2 is a sum of two bent functions; the function x₁x₂(x₃x₄ + x₅x₆ + x₇x₈) on 𝔽₂⁸ is a counterexample, and the paper's family gives one in every even dimension n ≥ 8. The derivative conjecture says that every balanced function of degree at most n/2 − 1 with a nonzero period is a derivative g(x) + g(x + a) of a bent function; x₂ + f₈(x₃, …, x₁₀) on 𝔽₂¹⁰ is a counterexample. The proof formalizes the paper's divisibility theorem (if x₁⋯x_k · s is a sum of two bent functions with 2k < n, then 2^(k+1) divides the weight of s), via Fourier inversion and Parseval on 𝔽₂ᵐ, and one direction of Shaporenko's lemma. A separate module proves that x₁x₂ + x₃x₄ + x₅x₆ + x₇x₈ is bent, so the definitions are not vacuous.",
